When an initial or rework leaf has already combined current trunk with its uncommitted work,
its tracked producer can run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate ELEMENT-ID`.
The command preserves the complete working candidate, replays checkpoint commits
with explicit signing in an isolated worktree, and advances the managed branch,
index and workspace metadata using a durable recovery intent. It does not merge
incoming source into the candidate: the producer must prepare that combination
before requesting recovery and validate the resulting candidate afterward.

The command requires a matching live producer and exclusive Story lease. It
accepts either a current rework revision or a provably absent first CR. Before
the first CR, scope comes from the authoritative board and the producer role
must match its configured archetype; corrupt or unreadable CR state refuses. It refuses staged changes, another pending Story revision,
wrong ownership, checkpoint replay conflicts, missing signatures, candidate drift,
and a moved protected authority. Explicit recovery supports nonfinal task-delta
leaves as well as final leaves; ordinary automatic refresh remains final-leaf-only
and still skips dirty
workspaces. Recovery leaves review state unchanged; publish a new revision through
normal handoff and obtain independent review before integration.

For conflicting regular files, the parent of the retained replay worktree contains
`resolution-template.json` with `version`, `branch_oid`, `trunk_oid` and
`candidate_tree_oid`. Copy that template to a task-scoped file and fill its
`resolutions` array. Each entry contains `checkpoint_oid` (the retained replay's
`git rev-parse REBASE_HEAD`), `path` (an actual unmerged repository-relative path),
`content` (base64 replacement bytes) and `sha256` (lowercase hex SHA-256 of those
bytes). Resolve the conflict deliberately; do not copy a newer final-leaf file
into an earlier checkpoint without checking its scope. Keep the resolution file
outside the candidate tree, under `.temp/`.


Candidate-preserving refresh also restores missing tracked files under
`.task-board/.resources/` when their blob identity matches the freshly verified
protected trunk. It never overwrites an existing file or backfills activity
history. Re-running `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-ID` with the
normal live producer lease repairs this resource projection even when the base
is already current. Staged changes and unsafe paths refuse; this does not
replace normal candidate validation or independent review.

Run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate ELEMENT-ID --replay-resolutions FILE`.
The command rebuilds the detached replay from the unchanged original checkpoints,
applies only exact checkpoint/path resolutions, and signs continuation. It
refuses stale bindings, unknown/duplicate/unused resolutions, digest mismatches,
unsafe paths and non-regular-file conflicts. The original working candidate
remains byte-for-byte intact. Preparation interruptions can be retried with the
same bound packet; existing durable intent resumes interrupted application.
Normal CR publication and independent review remain required.

Retry the same command after an interrupted branch/index or metadata update.
Provisioning retains a preserved-candidate transaction for the successor producer
instead of treating it as a completed clean rebase. Git scratch replay worktrees
remain under `.temp/base-refresh/` for recovery evidence; durable intent lives in
