  7945	resume:
  7946	
  7947	- each required repository or managed directory;
  7948	- sanitized remote URLs;
  7949	- HEAD object ID and symbolic branch, or detached state;
  7950	- reachable local commits needed for HEAD and submodules;
  7951	- staged index state;
  7952	- unstaged tracked content;
  7953	- untracked non-ignored content;
  7954	- configured agent project files even when ignored, subject to exclusions;
  7955	- recursive submodule identity, commit, and working state;
  7956	- repository-relative cwd; and
  7957	- the workspace-group sharing/worktree policy.
  7958	
  7959	Ignored build outputs are excluded by default. An ignored file is included only
  7960	when named by a project/provider include rule and classified as non-secret.
  7961	M1 Git closure is complete only when tracked, dirty-index, staged, unstaged,
  7962	untracked, ignored-policy, symlink, and submodule state is represented and
  7963	validated. Omitting any one class is <code>workspace_conflict</code>; a clean
  7964	HEAD or object pack is not a proxy for working-copy closure.
  7965	
  7966	### 12.2 Git workspace schema
  7967	
  7968	The Git wire schema is the exact <code>kind = git</code>
  7969	<code>WorkspaceSnapshotMember</code> and its closed nested types in Section
  7970	10.4. There is no second prose-only Git descriptor. In particular,
  7971	<code>head</code> distinguishes branch, detached, and unborn states;
  7972	<code>object_pack</code> and its inventory carry offline object closure; the raw
  7973	<code>index</code> blob plus logical <code>GitIndexEntry</code> array preserve
  7974	stages and flags; <code>working_tree_manifest_id</code> carries actual tracked,
  7975	unstaged, untracked, and explicitly included ignored bytes; and submodule,
  7976	feature, cwd, and agent-config fields are all required members of that same
  7977	Transfer Manifest object.
  7978	
  7979	Remote URLs MUST be sanitized. User info containing a password/token, credential
  7980	helpers with embedded secrets, and machine-local paths MUST be removed.
  7981	Repository config MUST be reconstructed from an allowlist: remotes, branch
  7982	tracking, object format, safe submodule URLs, and required worktree extensions.
  7983	Hooks, credential configuration, local include files, and arbitrary commands
  7984	MUST NOT be copied.
  7985	
  7986	The <code>WS-GIT-ROUNDTRIP-1</code> and
  7987	<code>WS-TREE-ROUNDTRIP-1</code> fixtures in Section 10.4 are normative inputs
  7988	to <code>AC-WORK-001</code>/<code>AC-WORK-002</code>. An implementation MUST
  7989	parse those exact descriptors before invoking Git or writing a destination;
  7990	it MUST reject their listed negative mutations without relying on a live
  7991	repository to fill missing schema facts.
  7992	
  7993	### 12.3 Git capture and materialization
  7994	
  7995	Capture MUST quiesce agent input and filesystem-mutating provider work. It MUST
  7996	read Git state consistently and fail if HEAD, index, or included file digests
  7997	change during capture.
  7998	
  7999	Materialization MUST:
  8000	
  8001	1. create or validate a managed repository/worktree;
  8002	2. configure sanitized remotes;
  8003	3. import each repository's object pack into that repository's object database
  8004	   only and verify its required OIDs there; a superproject gitlink is validated
  8005	   against the child descriptor/pack, never by co-mingling child objects into
  8006	   the parent database;
  8007	4. establish the exact branch or detached HEAD without contacting a remote;
  8008	5. construct a temporary index from the descriptor and atomically install it;
  8009	6. materialize actual working-tree bytes, modes, and symlinks;
  8010	7. recurse through submodules using their logical mappings; and
  8011	8. compare resulting HEAD, index, status, cwd, and content digests with the
  8012	   descriptor.
  8013	
  8014	An implementation MAY use the Git executable, a Go library, or both, but the
  8015	observable result is fixed by this section. Unsupported Git extensions, case
  8016	collisions, inaccessible filters, or object-format mismatches MUST fail closed.
  8017	Network fetch MAY be offered as an explicit repair, but materialization MUST NOT
  8018	silently depend on it.
  8019	
  8020	Staged and unstaged states are distinct: the reconstructed index represents the
  8021	staged version, while the working-tree manifest represents actual working
  8022	bytes. A clean-looking working tree with a wrong index is a failed
  8023	materialization.
  8024	For a submodule, the superproject HEAD-tree pointer, stage-0 index
  8025	<code>gitlink_oid</code>, and checked-out <code>head.oid</code> MUST reproduce the
  8026	exact clean/staged/unstaged combination in Section 10.4.
  8027	
